Predicate: {{predicate}}
Object: {{object}}

Response context: {{response}}

Does this triplet bundle two or more facts that can each be verified on their own?

Split ONLY when the predicate is true of each part separately:
(dog, eats, 'meat and vegetables') -> (dog, eats, 'meat') + (dog, eats, 'vegetables')

Keep it when the parts belong together: 'Alice and Bob are siblings', 'Anna and Tom got married', 'the fee is the same for members and guests', 'a mix of red and white', a claim reported inside the object, a hedge, a range or a value in alternative units, stacked adjectives, or a fixed compound name. When unsure, keep.

Split only at an explicit list, never cut one noun phrase into its words. Every part must be a sensible, grammatical claim on its own; if one is not, keep.

Never paraphrase, never swap subject and object, never take a fact from the context.

Return JSON in exactly this shape:
{{schema}}

The fields:
  reasoning  - one sentence on why you kept or split it
  is_atomic  - true to keep, false to split
  split      - the atomic triplets when is_atomic is false, otherwise an empty array
